  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>(a) When gas is adsorbed on metal surface.<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> become less negative with progress of reaction because number of active sites decreases.<br />(b) Gas with greater value of critical temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub></mfenced></math> absorbed more. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></math> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> absorbed more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>.</p><p>Statement (c) is wrong because residual forces decrease as adsorption proceeds further.</p><p>Statement (d) is also wrong because temperature opposes extent of adsorption.</p>",
